course: dictionary
lesson: 3312
title: Soft words and hidden meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A euphemism is a gentle, polite word or phrase that we use instead of a word that sounds too harsh, rude or upsetting. «Passed away» is a euphemism for «died», because it sounds softer.
explain: People use euphemisms to be kind, to be polite, or because a subject feels embarrassing. «Under the weather» is a soft way to say someone feels ill, and «let go» is a soft way to say someone lost their job.
explain: Some euphemisms hide the truth. Always ask yourself: what is the plain word this is hiding? If someone says a worker was «let go», the plain word might be «sacked». Read the whole sentence and the clues around it to find out.
fact: A euphemism is a soft or roundabout word used instead of a plain word that feels too hard, rude or sad.
fact: «Passed away» is a euphemism for «died», and «under the weather» is a euphemism for «ill».
fact: Euphemisms can hide unpleasant facts, so officials sometimes use them to make bad news sound better.
fact: Euphemisms for death can comfort a family, honour soldiers, sell funerals or make dark jokes.
fact: Not every euphemism is meant to be kind; some are meant to hide or to sound tough.
exercise: «Grandma passed away last year.» What plain word does «passed away» stand for? | *died | sang | forgot | moved house
exercise: «I'm feeling under the weather today.» What does this mean? | Feeling very windy | Feeling very happy | *Feeling a bit ill | Standing in the rain
exercise: The shop said Sam was «let go» in the sale. What does it really mean? | Sam won a prize | Sam was given a new shop | Sam went on holiday | *Sam lost his job
test: Why do people use euphemisms about the body and illness? | To shout louder so everyone hears | To be rude on purpose | *To be polite, protect someone's feelings, or because the subject feels embarrassing
test: «Not every euphemism is kind.» What might a euphemism be used for instead? | To say something in a foreign language | *To hide an unpleasant truth or to sound tough | To make a joke about the weather
exam: Officials said the cuts were a «rebalancing» of the budget. What is this euphemism likely trying to do? | *Make bad news sound better | Count the money twice | Show that the budget is huge | Explain a sum of money in detail
